    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Introduction: Postpartum mood disorders (PPMDs) are significant contributors of maternal morbidity and mortality. Many factors have the potential to negatively impact birth experiences, such as birth trauma, medicalized births, the patient-provider relationship, and the patient’s own health and personal background. “Good Birth” is a framework for understanding components of the labor and delivery process that could be used as targets for improving birth experiences.\r\n\r\nObjectives: 1) To understand the aspects underlying a “Good Birth” and generate a model describing the interaction of these aspects with potential birth trauma or medicalized pregnancy and 2) Explore how the interactions between aspects of “Good Birth”, birth trauma, medicalized pregnancy, the patient-provider relationship, and the individual patient’s lived experiences, identities, vulnerabilities, and SDOH can decrease the likelihood of PPMDs. \r\n\r\nMethods: Performed an integrative review by developing search terms and conducting a search across PubMed and Web of Science. After the review of 1,085 articles, eighteen articles were evaluated for quality and analyzed for the presence of “Good Birth” components, birth trauma, medicalized pregnancies, and the presence of any PPMD. \r\n\r\nResults: The Perinatal Mood Dynamics Model explores the potential ways that patients’ lived experiences, identities, vulnerabilities, and SDOH interact with the “Good Birth” components, patient-provider relationship, and medicalization of pregnancy/birth. The model also looks at how the patient’s self-appraisal of the birth experience further interacts with one’s protective and risk factors and leaves the patient either more vulnerable to or more protected from PPMDs. \r\n\r\nConclusion: “Good Birth” provides an integrated and intersectional framework that may be useful to providers in cultivating a positive, person-centered birth experience and decreasing the likelihood for PPMDs to develop in patient populations.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
